@page "/about"
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "About";
}
<h1>About this site</h1>
<p class="lede">
    <a href="https://linked.art/">Linked Art</a> is a community data model for describing art and cultural heritage, built on
    CIDOC-CRM and published as JSON-LD. <a href="https://github.com/tomcrane/linked-art-net">LinkedArtNet</a> is a .NET
    implementation of Linked Art 1.0: a typed model with a fluent API, a free-text date parser, a parsing service, and this site.
</p>

<h2>Why C#?</h2>
<p>
    JSON-LD examples are precise but hard to read in bulk. The same example as fluent C# reads like a description of the thing:
    a painting, produced by someone, at some time, carrying a title. Every example here is checked against the JSON that
    linked.art publishes, so the code you read is known to produce the real thing. Where it does not, the page says so and
    links the upstream issue.
</p>

<h2>Using the library</h2>
<p>Packages are not yet on NuGet; build from source. The repository README has the quick start. The libraries target .NET 10.</p>
<pre><code class="language-csharp">var painting = new HumanMadeObject("https://example.org/object/1")
    .WithLabel("Night Watch")
    .ClassifiedAs(Aat.Painting, Aat.WorkOfArt);
painting.ProducedBy().CarriedOutBy(rembrandt).WithTimeSpan("1642");</code></pre>

<h2 id="canonical">Canonical date expressions</h2>
<p>
    The date parser reduces every input to a small canonical language before producing a TimeSpan: EDTF where EDTF can say it
    (<code>1957/1959</code>, <code>1850~</code>), and a few keywords where it cannot (<code>late 195X</code>, <code>century 19</code>,
    <code>born 1606</code>, <code>fl 1480/1510</code>, <code>period:edo</code>, <code>4.5 Ga ago</code>). The same canonical form
    always realises to the same TimeSpan under a given profile, which is what makes the corpus testable. The language is documented in
    <a href="https://github.com/tomcrane/linked-art-net/blob/de-pmc/docs/canonical-expression.md">docs/canonical-expression.md</a>.
</p>

<h2>Profiles</h2>
<p>
    Conventions differ: "19th century" is 1800 to 1899 in a museum label and 1801 to 1900 to a medievalist; "early" is a third to
    some and a half to others; US archives write month first. A <strong>profile</strong> fixes these choices. The repository ships
    <code>default</code>, <code>library</code>, <code>archive-us</code>, <code>catalogue-quarters</code>, <code>medieval-manuscripts</code>
    and <code>multilingual</code>, and a profile advisor that proposes settings from a sample of your strings.
</p>

<h2 id="corpus">The corpus and its sources</h2>
<p>Every entry in the corpus states where it came from. Imported collections, each under a licence compatible with this project's MIT licence:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>yearrange</strong> (MIT), Christopher Johnson's year-range parser test data.</li>
    <li><strong>timetwister</strong> (MIT), the Yale date normalisation gem's specification examples.</li>
    <li><strong>Getty ULAN</strong> (ODC-By), life dates from the Union List of Artist Names, fetched from the Getty SPARQL endpoint.</li>
    <li><strong>EDTF specification</strong> (public domain), the Library of Congress's examples.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    Named period tables (Japanese eras, Chinese dynasties, British periods, archaeological ages) and the seven language lexicons
    state their provenance inside each file. Full notices are in
    <a href="https://github.com/tomcrane/linked-art-net/blob/de-pmc/corpus/imported/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md">THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md</a>.
</p>

